WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.900 align:start position:0%
 
father  visitor was not pleased  hey everyone is Peter Brown standing in  for freelancer Daniel Starkey for their  review of Warhammer 40,000 Dawn of War 3  Donal war 3 is a game at odds with  itself matches start with a lot of  momentum and expand quickly before  settling into a soft balance for long  stretches careful control of elite  warriors on the frontlines is essential  but so is constantly nurturing your base  and marshaling upgrades for your armies  despite that Donna war 3 holds its own  offering up delicious tooth and nail  fights that will push both your  technical skill and strategic aptitude  to their limit like most games under the  Warhammer umbrella Donna war 3 isn't  that friendly to newcomers and it  actually matters this time around  everything from tactical options to unit  tactics means at least partially on  knowledge of the Warhammer universe the  tutorial will do a solid job of giving  you the tools you need to get going but  background knowledge is all but  essential beyond a healthy addition to  the already massive Warhammer canon the  single-player campaign doesn't offer  much each of its 17 missions serve as a  drawn-out tutorial and that's ok on its  own but without interesting twists on  the fundamentals of play you're better  off starting with multiplayer online  skirmishes can have between 2 and 6  human or AI players split into two teams  each is charged with defending a  powerful both sides start with an array  of basic defences and from there you'll  plan out your base and capture strategic  points around the map - pulling  resources and keep tabs on the enemy  that in itself could form the backbone  of the game but dawn of war 3 also has  an array of powerful hero units each is  a pillar of the Warhammer story and  comes with weapons and powers befitting  their esteem  we've completed a power generator  while most elite says they're called can  turn the time on their own they're akin  to a queen and chess and that if you do  manage to lose one it can be devastating  what this means practically speaking is  that just as your base building gets  more complex and requires more care and  attention  you're also tasked with tactically  managing your elite units that makes for  one steep learning curve but for those  that manage it there's a lot of added  depth overall there's an unusual  attentiveness to the frontlines which  lends itself to white-knuckle play  pressing with the gargantuan elite as a  distraction right as you capture a  resource node is exhilarating and made  that much better when it results in  activating a means to warp in  reinforcements from across the map these  gains are always tenuous though Donna  voir strictly limits army size and  resource gathering slows exponentially  with the more troops you gain this means  that even if you knock an opponent down  they'll build up resources quickly and  come back swinging in short order that  magnifies the importance of  psychological play running with the  previous example while you'd have a  strong forward position that also  stretches supply lines and leaves you  open to a swift brutal counter-attack  these quick reversals are brilliant and  make for intense memorable matches while  humans are far more fun to spar with the  included AI isn't a slouch they will try  their own tricks often hinting at larger  enemies than they actually have if only  to intimidate you and launch a sneak  attack to your core base each of the  three main factions also compliment one  another well in the classic  rock-paper-scissors fashion Space  Marines are slow to build up momentum  but once they hit the field they're a  force elder are mobile and suited for  hit and run attacks in the orcs well  they're weird they are exceptionally  strong but only when they admit a  war-cry which while terrifying notifies  everyone in the field to put their  garden in the middle of matches you can  tune your army a bit changing out  different elite units as a kind of  lowdown in a nod to its tabletop  inspirations you can also customize the  paint and color scheme for every unit in  the game given the role of army  customization to war hammer proper it's  a shame you can't also swap out weapons  and gear for your basic units and  vehicles the options given help mix  things up a bit but they're also a bit  shell and a few other problems lurk here  and there particularly in the user  interface  on multiple occasions elite units  wouldn't deselect when clicking around  the map at times dragging boxes around  troops wouldn't highlight them at all  while small problems on the whole they  did cause their share of raised voices  Dawn of War 3 certainly sounds amazing  with crisp sound effects and an  excellent soundtrack but unfortunately  the visual presentation leaves something  to be desired battles often look great  zoomed out but pulling in shows plenty  of blemishes the camera also doesn't do  a good job of showing off the  battlefield even at its most distant  very little of the map fits on the  screen meaning that you can expect to  need to move around a lot during play an  odd chimera of its forebears there's a  lot in this fast-paced RTS that is just  a little bit off parts of the interface  don't work sometimes inter match army  management feels half-baked and  micromanagement needed to use the game's  signature hero units effectively doesn't  jive with the extensive base building  you need to support them even so dawn of  war 3 is a rare strategy game that keeps  the adrenaline pumping throughout  you

